        'Physics, hydrology, slope mechanics, and trophic chemistry are incorruptibly blind to human insignia. Yet across Kashmir and the wider Western Himalayas, public and institutional environmental consciousness rarely exhibits that blindness. Again and again, civic outrage rises to a roar when an ecologically destructive act is committed by a political rival, a distant bureaucracy, or an unfamiliar constituency—and drops to an awkward whisper when identical physical damage is inflicted by one’s own social circle, favoured party, commercial guild, or revered institution. Closing the twenty-one-dispatch Inaugural Edition of Himalayan Dispatch, we confront the hardest mirror before environmentalism itself: can environmental advocacy retain its moral authority if ecological standards change according to who is developing the landscape?',
